{"title":"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_14\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 14\">2025 AMC 10B #14</a> and <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2025 AMC 12B #11</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_14\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 14</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Counting)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Counting)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(similar_to_Solution_3)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (similar to Solution 3)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(cheese)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (cheese)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7_(Fast,_like_Solution_5)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7 (Fast, like Solution 5)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solultion_8\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solultion 8</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Solution_9_(Combinatorial_Counting)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 9 (Combinatorial Counting)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:00)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:00)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Fast_and_Easy)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Fast and Easy)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_14\">Problem 14</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 14\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Nine athletes, no two of whom are the same height, try out for the basketball team. One at a time, they draw a wristband at random, without replacement, from a bag containing 3 blue bands, 3 red bands, and 3 green bands. They are divided into a blue group, a red group, and a green group. The tallest member of each group is named the group captain. What is the probability that the group captains are the three tallest athletes?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/9/ac9e8fceabf6582c06fab6ac93f5291ae52cbf98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } \\dfrac{2}{9} \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } \\dfrac{2}{7} \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } \\dfrac{9}{28} \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } \\dfrac{1}{3}  \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } \\dfrac{3}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"384\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We will arrange the nine athletes in a line, with the first three being in the blue group, the next three being in the red group, and the last three being in the green group. Suppose the three tallest athletes are named <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299ddf7c0f03ea46bf09f81f93adeea0756aa15c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" />, in some order. \n</p><p>We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/6859713adc973d3fbe97fafef9ce829757e54b12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> ways to choose which group each of these athletes can be in. We then have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/6/736d126c150068f71f8cee76a12b549dbef2fbf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" /> ways to determine which of the three positions in that group they can take. From here, we must distribute the remaining six athletes in the remaining six spaces, which can be done in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/6/e26ca46a67d19ce2e5b692103305a083a2361fd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> ways.\n</p><p>There are therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/d/3fdca54e328ea20b6528a02256f94b44ae399b8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3! \\cdot 27 \\cdot 6!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"75\" height=\"14\" /> favorable outcomes. There are also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/f/22f925a6f7de3d6e06f740617bc11b55a1f97184.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> total ways to arrange the athletes with no restrictions. Our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/b/92b29cd48b1c6f084917944dc9399404813d7e4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3! \\cdot 27 \\cdot 6!}{9!} = \\frac{6 \\cdot 27}{9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{9}{28}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"258\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~lprado\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the three tallest athletes be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> (tallest), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a8ae66ded7f33f9385a37c9cadfb82720013d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> (second-tallest), and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/1/791fe65c9d1ed7d8ed33a5025f40c5c7f9e476af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> (third-tallest).\n</p><p>The condition \"the captains are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/e/ebe25419750d7bf54ef7ea727965769edfccdce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1, A_2, \\text{and } A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"114\" height=\"18\" />\" is equivalent to the condition \"<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/e/ebe25419750d7bf54ef7ea727965769edfccdce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1, A_2, \\text{and } A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"114\" height=\"18\" /> must all be in different-colored groups.\" We can find this probability by considering them drawing bands sequentially. \nAthlete <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> (Tallest): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> draws a band. It can be any of the 9 bands. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> will always be a captain, so the probability is 1. We now have 8 bands left.\nAthlete <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a8ae66ded7f33f9385a37c9cadfb82720013d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> (2nd Tallest): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a8ae66ded7f33f9385a37c9cadfb82720013d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> must draw a band of a different color than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" />.\nThere are 8 bands left in the bag.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> took one band, so 2 bands of that color remain.\nThe other 6 bands (3 of one color, 3 of another) are \"good\" bands for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a8ae66ded7f33f9385a37c9cadfb82720013d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />.\nThe probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a8ae66ded7f33f9385a37c9cadfb82720013d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> drawing a different color is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/f/aaf85a6b7a4493fb26f071f880c01ae29c27eaa8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(A_2) = \\frac{6}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"89\" height=\"38\" />. Athlete <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/1/791fe65c9d1ed7d8ed33a5025f40c5c7f9e476af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> (3rd Tallest): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/1/791fe65c9d1ed7d8ed33a5025f40c5c7f9e476af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> must draw a band of the third, remaining color.\nWe assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a8ae66ded7f33f9385a37c9cadfb82720013d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> were successful, so there are 7 bands left.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> took a band of color 1. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a8ae66ded7f33f9385a37c9cadfb82720013d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> took a band of color 2.\nThere are 3 bands of color 3 (the \"good\" color) still in the bag.\nThe probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/1/791fe65c9d1ed7d8ed33a5025f40c5c7f9e476af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> drawing the last color is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/2/fb289906516bdf820c38297125732e4caf49066a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(A_3) = \\frac{3}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"89\" height=\"38\" />.\nThe total probability is the product of these independent probabilities:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/1/cb167520095febfb42b5595d2d72806d3383a015.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P(\\text{Total}) = P(A_1) \\times P(A_2) \\times P(A_3)\\]\" width=\"281\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/f/a0faf9b5be206eb73d734df90524743e37cb3329.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P(\\text{Total}) = 1 \\times \\frac{6}{8} \\times \\frac{3}{7} = \\frac{18}{56}\\]\" width=\"214\" height=\"37\" />\nSimplifying the fraction:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/0/0b076f3f9b0bf3d984f2387cbbe11a1b74d93ea3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{18}{56} = \\frac{9}{28}\\]\" width=\"65\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Thus, the correct option is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/d/6fd8a607b075709acb63be27f8d1d519dfe88cfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{9}{28}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"75\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~Jonathanmo\n</p><p>~Minor edits by JESSIE_XIE (formalized the mathematical expressions in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/latex.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\LaTeX$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Counting.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Counting)\">Solution 3 (Counting)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Counting)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/8/858f284e528cb7e5897d426afcc5d359cd2b41d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{9}{3}\\binom{6}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"76\" height=\"55\" /> ways to assign rubber bands to the athletes. Now we have to find the number of ways for the team captains to be the 3 tallest people. Each of the 3 tallest people must have a different rubber band color, and there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/b/4db3b59940e4f5cd42bbab2b7815c452a0197507.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3! = 6$\" width=\"47\" height=\"13\" /> ways to permute their individual rubber band colors.\n</p><p>For the other 6 people, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/4/c7496acbb8ca7b1c0019575b7828e1efb0f88f35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{6}{2}\\binom{4}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"76\" height=\"55\" /> ways to assign rubber bands to them. \n</p><p>Thus, our probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/a/a2acf8b4a3931dc392e1e6681090aa059f4a1250.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3! \\cdot \\binom{6}{2}\\binom{4}{2}}{\\binom{9}{3}\\binom{6}{3}} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\hspace{3pt}\\frac{9}{28}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"170\" height=\"51\" />.\n</p><p>~Milkdrinker\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28similar_to_Solution_3.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(similar_to_Solution_3)\">Solution 4 (similar to Solution 3)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (similar to Solution 3)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/b/b4bf01e9639c1a7e6ffeb2ff5d74b07f7606259a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{9}{3}\\cdot \\binom{6}{3}\\cdot \\binom{3}{3}=1680$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"200\" height=\"55\" /> ways to select the people in each team, and we must divide by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/6859713adc973d3fbe97fafef9ce829757e54b12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> ways to order the teams. This gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/c/b0c1e852fa247404cd2543382c65a14f7cb3608b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1680}{3!}=280$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"94\" height=\"38\" /> total ways to create the teams.\n</p><p>To have the three team captains be the tallest, we need them to be in separate teams. Once placed in their respective teams we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/b/4db3b59940e4f5cd42bbab2b7815c452a0197507.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3! = 6$\" width=\"47\" height=\"13\" /> ways to orient them.  We then have 6 people remaining. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/a/2fa8c841e922bb16f3cdd0ada50627f1c7990d20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{6}{2}\\cdot \\binom{4}{2}\\cdot \\binom{2}{2} = 90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"182\" height=\"55\" /> ways to place two more people on each team. However, this overcounts by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/2/3920c5e3f6b2e5749cd75c3853b63ae714d00561.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3!=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" />, as the orientation of the teams themselves doesn't matter, hence in reality only 15 cases work. We multiply by the orientation of each athlete, which yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/3/7433aedb769dfcb0c65eb4293971430547b21dd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15 \\cdot 6 = 90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"85\" height=\"14\" /> cases.\n</p><p>Our probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/e/4de2718fdc7a1d13d0d1f39f6ea74dd56b52a3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{90}{280} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)} \\frac{9}{28}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"119\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~OlympiadMaster\n</p><p>~Huge edit by Pinotation (Logical Error in Solution)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Assume the tallest person is in the red group. Then the second person has 6 spots to go out of 8 remaining (1 was taken away). Then the third tallest person has 3 spots to go out of 7 remaining. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/a/04a8851da2eb3e7fa62e9ea764ef6a55d78d8109.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"41\" height=\"38\" /> is the same as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/d/6fd8a607b075709acb63be27f8d1d519dfe88cfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{9}{28}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"75\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~fastone\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28cheese.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(cheese)\">Solution 6 (cheese)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (cheese)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Upon realizing that the denominator is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and that there are a bunch of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s in the numerator (just do some C&amp;P with the right logic that could even be a bit flawed) we can make an educated guess that the answer must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/d/6fd8a607b075709acb63be27f8d1d519dfe88cfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{9}{28}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"75\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~vaishnav\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7_.28Fast.2C_like_Solution_5.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7_(Fast,_like_Solution_5)\">Solution 7 (Fast, like Solution 5)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7 (Fast, like Solution 5)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>WLOG, assume that the three tallest people are the first ones to draw. Irrespective of where the first person goes, the probability that the second and third person go on different teams is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/3/f23777c303ab438127c033783d95c2e0800402af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/8/758a4c1c67be9c9db0c4a808c62384d660579448.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />, respectively, yielding\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/d/6fd8a607b075709acb63be27f8d1d519dfe88cfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{9}{28}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"75\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~AlgeBruh16\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solultion_8\">Solultion 8</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solultion 8\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can visualize the nine athletes as a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d278803d72fcc493afb1559174a483aa7f41d143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> grid, where each row represents a color group and each row contains 3 positions. We want the three tallest athletes to become captains. This happens exactly when each of the three tallest athletes occupies a different row.\n</p><p>Total ways to choose positions for the three tallest athletes: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/e/d4e642f12ae500ab0f96f0f15c8d90bdfdbf4eea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{9}{3} = 84$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"79\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Favorable ways (exactly one of the three tallest athletes in each row): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/0/7505050e0034432d1b03c656b92f39f0f04215e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^3=27$\" width=\"59\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>Thus, our probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/e/39e5e45a376d5259bd5a4ad66afd864bee81fcbd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{27}{84} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{9}{28}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"122\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~MathKing555\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_9_.28Combinatorial_Counting.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_9_(Combinatorial_Counting)\">Solution 9 (Combinatorial Counting)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 9 (Combinatorial Counting)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The three tallest athletes must be in three different groups. Otherwise, if two of them were in the same group, the shorter one could not be a captain. Form the blue group first. Choose 1 of the 3 tallest athletes and 2 of the other 6 athletes:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/4/2e4f7895528a2b637dea85eb1927186d5b672227.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{3 \\choose 1}\\cdot{6 \\choose 2}\\]\" width=\"79\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>For the red group, choose 1 of the remaining 2 tallest athletes and 2 of the remaining 4 athletes:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/2/e223c0fbe87f6956830b59770f1fec0dc5ef0eef.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{2 \\choose 1}\\cdot{4 \\choose 2}\\]\" width=\"79\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>The remaining 3 athletes form the green group. Thus, the number of favorable groupings is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/4/05477bf08e4d3f108517adbb5b5d5ed0bdf3c96c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{3 \\choose 1}\\cdot{6 \\choose 2}\\cdot{2 \\choose 1}\\cdot{4 \\choose 2}=540.\\]\" width=\"239\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>The total number of ways to divide the 9 athletes into the three labeled groups is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/b/8fbdf5c12d2c771b61af22c89b2d3ced8bb650d9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{9 \\choose 3}\\cdot{6 \\choose 3}=1680.\\]\" width=\"147\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, the desired probability is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/3/14360adbbb9e83efebcbc107d6f3f27e8f21534b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{540}{1680}=\\frac{9}{28}.\\]\" width=\"88\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Thus,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/2/cf20ba12a8c97e5b9e8f115b3213a553fa1f31e0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{9}{28}}.\\]\" width=\"72\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>~JESSIE_XIE\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_.28Logical.2C_Good_Editing.2C_.7E1:00.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:00)\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:00)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:00)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtube.com/shorts/85Sa75b8Z-g?si=rRl9ZIXDtO15mHI_\">https://youtube.com/shorts/85Sa75b8Z-g?si=rRl9ZIXDtO15mHI_</a>\n</p><p>~Ebai\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution.28Quick_and_Simple.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution(Quick and Simple)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a-VZKx9dKbo\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a-VZKx9dKbo</a>\n~Sigma_Bob\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Fast_and_Easy.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Fast_and_Easy)\">Video Solution (Fast and Easy)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Fast and Easy)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-AZDidEroIo\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-AZDidEroIo</a>\n~ Pi Academy\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dLSWVrrKsgs\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dLSWVrrKsgs</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B\">2025 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 13\">Problem 13</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 15\">Problem 15</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_2\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_4\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_8\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\